    "outcome_summary": "The study analyzed PIRLS 2016 data for 3,959 fourth-grade students in Germany and compared metropolitan, urban, and rural regions using weighted OLS, bootstrapping, multiple imputation, PCA, and LASSO-based post-selection regressions. In baseline models, rural and metropolitan students were more likely than urban students to receive an academic track recommendation, and they also reported higher enjoyment of school; rural students showed higher reading interest in the baseline specification. After adding selected student-level characteristics, most regional differences shrank substantially or disappeared, suggesting that compositional differences in student and family background explained much of the regional gap. The strongest explanatory variables were student-level background, skills, and school-related experience measures, while school-level variables added comparatively little beyond noise.",
